---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const mainFormula = String.raw`\log_2 m = D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P\|U_m)+H(P)`;
const samples = [
  { m: 2, cap: 1.0, dkl: 1.0, h: 0.0 },
  { m: 6, cap: 2.585, dkl: 1.585, h: 1.0 },
  { m: 30, cap: 4.907, dkl: 3.252, h: 1.655 },
  { m: 210, cap: 7.714, dkl: 4.916, h: 2.798 },
];
const maxCap = 8;
const chart = { left: 58, right: 720, top: 32, bottom: 300 };
const barWidth = 66;
const y = (value: number) => chart.bottom - (value / maxCap) * (chart.bottom - chart.top);
const barX = (index: number) => 105 + index * 155;
const principles = [
  {
    tag: 'capacity',
    title: 'Total capacity is fixed',
    body: 'For $m$ possibilities, there is a total budget of distinctions. In technical language, that budget is $\\log_2 m$ bits.',
  },
  {
    tag: 'persistence',
    title: 'Structure is distance from uniformity',
    body: '$D_{\\mathrm{KL}}$ measures what distinguishes the actual distribution from the maximally undifferentiated state.',
  },
  {
    tag: 'entropy',
    title: 'The remainder stays as uncertainty',
    body: '$H(P)$ measures what remains dispersed, uncrystallized, not selected as persistent structure.',
  },
];

const beginnerConcepts = [
  {
    title: 'A constraint',
    body: 'A constraint is a rule that prevents everything from happening. In a sieve, some numbers disappear; in a physical system, some configurations become impossible.',
  },
  {
    title: 'What persists',
    body: 'What persists is not what merely stays motionless. It is what passes through a constraint without losing its structural identity.',
  },
  {
    title: 'What disperses',
    body: 'Everything that is not stabilized remains as uncertainty, open choice, or noise. PT calls this the residual entropy.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The total budget',
    body: 'The key point is that structure and dispersion do not add freely. They share one same budget of distinction.',
  },
];

const plainFlow = [
  { label: 'possibilities', value: 'what could happen' },
  { label: 'constraint', value: 'what sorts' },
  { label: 'persistence', value: 'what keeps a form' },
  { label: 'entropy', value: 'what remains open' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The fundamental principle of persistence"
  description="A mathematics page on GFT, the identity log2(m)=DKL+H, and its role as the fundamental principle of the Theory of Persistence."
>
  <section class="border-b border-slate-200 bg-white">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 md:py-16">
      <p class="font-sans text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-derorange mb-3">
        PT mathematics / master identity
      </p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-thmblue leading-tight mb-5">
        The fundamental principle of persistence
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-700 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl" data-max-depth="L1">
        PT starts from a simple idea: when a constraint acts, not everything
        disappears. One part becomes structure; another part remains dispersed.
        The first principle says that these two parts are not independent:
        they share one same total amount of distinction.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-700 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl" data-min-depth="L2">
        GFT is the identity that formalizes this budget. For any distribution
        {String.raw`$P$`} over {String.raw`$m$`} states, total capacity
        {String.raw`$\log_2 m$`} decomposes exactly into persistent information
        and residual entropy.
      </p>
      <div data-min-depth="L2" class="mt-7 max-w-3xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg px-4 py-4 font-mono text-slate-900 overflow-x-auto">
        {`$${mainFormula}$`}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section data-max-depth="L1" class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr] gap-8 items-start">
      <div>
        <p class="font-sans text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-derorange mb-3">
          Plain language
        </p>
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-4">
          Before the formula
        </h2>
        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          Imagine a sorting process. At first, many things are possible. Then a
          rule acts: it removes, separates, or orients. At the end, there are
          two things to read: what has taken a stable form, and what remains
          still open.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          For PT, this is persistence. It does not mean “staying the same
          without moving.” It means surviving an admissible constraint while
          keeping a readable identity. The technical formula comes later; it
          counts this distribution.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        {beginnerConcepts.map((item) => (
          <article class="pt-card">
            <h3 class="font-sans text-lg font-bold text-thmblue mb-2">{item.title}</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">{item.body}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[0.88fr_1.12fr] gap-8 items-center mb-12">
      <div>
        <span class="pt-tag pt-tag-bridge">Intuition</span>
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mt-4 mb-4">
          One budget, two ways to occupy it
        </h2>
        <div data-max-depth="L1">
          <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The word “information” can sound intimidating, but here it first
            means: how many differences do you need to recognize a situation?
            If everything looks the same, there is little to distinguish. If a
            form appears, something becomes recognizable.
          </p>
          <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The first principle says that this recognizability has a cost. The
            sharper a structure becomes, the less blur remains available inside
            the same system. If the structure dissolves, the blur rises again.
          </p>
          <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            This is the thread linking the other pages: in cosmogony, the
            cascade sorts thresholds; in the periodic table, shells and blocks
            are stabilized forms; here, we explain the common accounting rule.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div data-min-depth="L2">
        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Picture a game with several possible squares. The more squares there
          are, the more distinctions you need to say exactly where you are.
          That total number of distinctions is the system’s budget.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Mathematically, that budget is written {String.raw`$\log_2(m)$`}
          because it is counted in yes/no questions, hence in bits. But the
          idea behind it is simply this: the system has a total amount of
          distinction available.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          One part becomes form: it distinguishes, constrains, selects. That is
          persistence. The other part remains open, dispersed, undecided. That
          is entropy.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          The fundamental principle says that the two parts compensate exactly. When
          structure gains one bit, available entropy loses one bit, and
          conversely. PT places that conservation at the center of the theory.
        </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div data-max-depth="L1" class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5">
        <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-4">Read it in four gestures</h3>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-4 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-3">
          {plainFlow.map((step, index) => (
            <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4">
              <span class="font-sans text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-derorange">0{index + 1}</span>
              <h4 class="font-sans font-bold text-slate-900 mt-2">{step.label}</h4>
              <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 mt-1">{step.value}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>

      <div data-min-depth="L2" class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5">
        <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-3">Information budget of the sieve</h3>
        <div class="mb-2 flex justify-end gap-4 text-sm font-sans text-slate-800">
          <span class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="w-4 h-4 rounded bg-thmblue inline-block"></span>persistence</span>
          <span class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="w-4 h-4 rounded bg-derorange inline-block"></span>entropy</span>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 760 360" class="w-full h-auto" role="img" aria-label="GFT budget at primorial levels">
          <rect x="0" y="0" width="760" height="360" rx="8" fill="#ffffff" />
          {[0,2,4,6,8].map((tick) => (
            <g>
              <line x1={chart.left} x2={chart.right} y1={y(tick)} y2={y(tick)} stroke="#e2e8f0" />
              <text x="24" y={y(tick) + 4} font-size="12" fill="#5f6f80" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui">{tick}</text>
            </g>
          ))}
          <text x="24" y="24" font-size="12" fill="#5f6f80" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui">bits</text>
          {samples.map((sample, index) => {
            const x0 = barX(index);
            const dklTop = y(sample.dkl);
            const capTop = y(sample.cap);
            return (
              <g>
                <rect x={x0} y={dklTop} width={barWidth} height={chart.bottom - dklTop} fill="#14566b" rx="4" />
                <rect x={x0} y={capTop} width={barWidth} height={dklTop - capTop} fill="#c96f24" rx="4" />
                <text x={x0 + barWidth / 2} y={chart.bottom + 26} text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#17202a" font-weight="700" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui">m={sample.m}</text>
                <text x={x0 + barWidth / 2} y={capTop - 8} text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#5f6f80" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui">{sample.cap.toFixed(2)}</text>
              </g>
            );
          })}
          <text x="390" y="344" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#5f6f80" font-family="ui-sans-serif, system-ui">
            each bar shows the same total budget: persistence + entropy
          </text>
        </svg>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-12">
      {principles.map((item) => (
        <div class="pt-card">
          <span class="pt-tag pt-tag-der">{item.tag}</span>
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue text-xl mt-3 mb-2">{item.title}</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div data-min-depth="L2" class="mb-12">
      <span class="pt-tag pt-tag-id">Standard</span>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mt-4 mb-4">
        Why this is a fundamental principle
      </h2>
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[1fr_0.9fr] gap-6 items-start">
        <div class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          <p class="mb-4">
            The identity is algebraic tautology: it is true for any
            distribution. Its force therefore does not come from technical
            difficulty, but from the conservation language it gives to PT.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-4">
            In the sieve, {String.raw`$D_{\mathrm{KL}}$`} becomes the structured
            part: what has been selected, constrained, made distinct.
            {String.raw`$H(P)$`} becomes the part still dispersed. Their sum
            remains the same information cap.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-sm text-slate-900 overflow-x-auto">
            {String.raw`$dD_{\mathrm{KL}}+dH=0,\quad \frac{dH}{dD_{\mathrm{KL}}}=-1$`}
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-5">
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-3">Exact status</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-slate-700">
            <li><strong>GFT-ID.</strong> Exact algebraic identity.</li>
            <li><strong>Arrow.</strong> Fixed-{String.raw`$m$`} identity: variations compensate.</li>
            <li><strong>Bekenstein.</strong> Immediate theorem: {String.raw`$D_{\mathrm{KL}}\le\log_2 m$`}.</li>
            <li><strong>Physical reading.</strong> Interpretive bridge treated elsewhere in the monograph.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div data-min-depth="L3">
      <span class="pt-tag pt-tag-id">Proof</span>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mt-4 mb-4">
        Complete proof
      </h2>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-6">
        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Let {String.raw`$P=(p_0,\ldots,p_{m-1})$`} be a probability
          distribution and let {String.raw`$U_m=(1/m,\ldots,1/m)$`} be the
          uniform distribution. By definition:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-sm text-slate-900 overflow-x-auto mb-4">
          {String.raw`$D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P\|U_m)=\sum_r p_r\log_2\frac{p_r}{1/m}$`}
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Distribute the logarithm, then use {String.raw`$\sum_r p_r=1$`}:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-sm text-slate-900 overflow-x-auto mb-4">
          {String.raw`$D_{\mathrm{KL}}=\sum_r p_r(\log_2 p_r+\log_2 m)=\log_2 m+\sum_r p_r\log_2 p_r$`}
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Since {String.raw`$H(P)=-\sum_r p_r\log_2 p_r$`}, we obtain:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-4 font-mono text-sm text-slate-900 overflow-x-auto">
          {String.raw`$D_{\mathrm{KL}}=\log_2 m-H(P)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \log_2 m=D_{\mathrm{KL}}+H(P)$`}
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-8 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div class="pt-card">
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-2">What this page claims</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">
            GFT is a universal identity, then a PT reading principle when its
            two terms are applied to the sieve and to residue distributions.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pt-card">
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue mb-2">What it does not claim</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">
            The identity alone does not prove a new law about primes. Its depth
            comes from its role in the PT chain, not from the algebra.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-sm font-sans">
        <a href="/en/theorems/GFT" class="rounded-md border border-slate-300 px-3 py-2 text-thmblue hover:bg-slate-50">GFT sheet</a>
        <a href="/en/theorems/T2" class="rounded-md border border-slate-300 px-3 py-2 text-thmblue hover:bg-slate-50">T2 · spectral conservation</a>
        <a href="/en/gaps-premiers" class="rounded-md border border-slate-300 px-3 py-2 text-thmblue hover:bg-slate-50">Prime gaps</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
